window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][50] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if return rates fall when you add exploration? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I'd reduce the share or make the exploration better matched (adjacent items), and check whether the fall is a novelty effect that fades. If it persists, the engagement cost is real and I'd decide with leadership using the long-term evidence.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Is narrowing always bad? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;No: for many users a focused taste is satisfying. It is a problem when it shrinks what a user would want to see if shown, and for creators who lose exposure. So I'd look at both user satisfaction and the catalog's reach.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c28-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C28-08 · Hard · product-sense · PS · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A watch notifies a wearer of a possible irregular heart rhythm. Design what happens after the alert.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Tell them to see a doctor.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives clear next steps and handles anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs a guided path using verified study facts, offers confirming options, uses plain language about what the alert means, measures follow-through and distress, and works within regulation and clinical input.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general and uses a published study; no internal claim. &quot;Facts from the published study: 0.52% of participants were notified; the notification had a positive predictive value of 0.84 against a simultaneous monitor patch, but only 34% of notified participants who wore a patch about two weeks later showed the condition, and the authors noted that the condition can come and go, so a later patch can miss it <code>[V: peer-reviewed paper, 2019]</code>. So a person who gets an alert has most likely had a real irregular rhythm, may not show it again soon, and needs to understand both.</p>\n<p>&quot;Design: plain language ('your watch noticed an irregular rhythm; this is not a diagnosis; it can be worth talking to a clinician'); an immediate choice of a confirming step: record a reading now if the device supports it, book a clinic or telehealth visit, or schedule a monitor; a summary the person can share with a clinician; no alarmist wording and clear urgent-symptom guidance (chest pain, fainting means seek emergency care). Measures: the share who take a confirming step within two weeks, the share who report distress, and the share of alerts followed by a diagnosis (reported with consent); guardrails: false-alert reports and unnecessary visits. Process: clinical review of wording, and regulatory consideration of claims (C27.9).&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Facts used correctly; a guided path; plain language; measures; clinical and regulatory care.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you avoid over-testing people who are fine? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By being clear about precision (most alerts are real, but some are not), offering the lowest-burden confirming step first (a recorded reading) and suggesting clinical care when it persists. I'd measure unnecessary visits as a guardrail.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What about people who never get an alert? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The design must not imply reassurance from silence: the study reported precision, not the share of true cases caught, so I'd say the feature does not detect all cases and does not replace regular care.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c28-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C28-09 · Intermediate · product-sense · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A bold redesign of search, launched as the company's biggest change in a decade, appears to be hurting users who know exactly what they want. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll it back.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by intent and measures the harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the inspiration and fixed-intent jobs, measures the cost to each with a holdout, keeps the default path for the common intent, adds the redesigned path alongside, sets a decision rule, and communicates openly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;First separate the jobs: guests who want inspiration and guests who know where and when they want to stay. Measure each: search-to-booking conversion, time to a booking, the number of steps, and complaints, by intent segment, with a holdout on the old search where possible. Public commentary on a similar redesign said it forced searching by category and confused location-bound searchers <code>[V: commentary, 2023; opinion]</code>, and the company redesigned its type-of-place filter in 2023 and, according to trade press, ended category search in 2025 <code>[V: Airbnb release; trade press]</code>. I'd treat those as hypotheses to check in our own data.</p>\n<p>&quot;If the harm to fixed-intent users is real, restore the classic path as the default and keep the new browsing path as an optional surface for people who want inspiration; test that combination against both groups. Decision rule: keep any element that raises inspiration-segment bookings without lowering fixed-intent conversion beyond a stated margin. Communicate openly to hosts and guests what is changing and why. A bold change that gets adjusted after learning is normal; the mistake is leaving a harmed segment unmeasured.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Intent segments; measured harm; a default path; a decision rule; openness.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Leadership called it the biggest change in a decade. How do you propose rolling back part of it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With the data and a framing of progress: we learned which segment benefits and which needs the classic path, and we are keeping the best of both. A change that is adjusted after evidence shows judgment; the data should lead the conversation.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you have tested it before launch? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By intent segment, with the new path offered as an option alongside the old one in a subset of markets, measuring the two groups separately, and with a rollback trigger on fixed-intent conversion.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c28-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Eight exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are invented unless stated as verified in the teardowns.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c28-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C28-01 · Goal-first statements</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a goal-first statement (FW-C11-02) for three products you use: a messaging app, a payments app and a fitness tracker. Include the criterion you would judge each by.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The first minute of any teardown.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C11-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Messaging app:</em> &quot;Exists to help people stay in touch reliably; for the company it earns retention and reach; I'll judge it on how quickly and reliably a message reaches the right person.&quot; <em>Payments app:</em> &quot;Exists to help people and small merchants move money safely and fast; for the company it earns fees and trust; I'll judge it on completion of a payment without error and the clarity of what went wrong when it fails.&quot; <em>Fitness tracker:</em> &quot;Exists to help people understand and change their activity; for the company it earns device sales and subscriptions; I'll judge it on whether users keep wearing it and change a behavior.&quot; Each states an assumption the interviewer can correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Write the statement as a goal for the user only, then add the company goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A committed goal focuses the critique and can be wrong; say it is an assumption.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c28-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C28-02 · Label the claims</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Label each claim in this paragraph as Verified (and name the source type you would need), Reconstructed, Observation, Inference or Opinion, and rewrite any you cannot support: &quot;The ride app obviously adds a surge price to make drivers happy. Fares rose 20% last year. The map is slow on my phone. It's the best app in the market.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Label discipline.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C11-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;Obviously adds a surge price to make drivers happy&quot;: an unsupported claim about intent; rewrite as an inference: &quot;I infer surge pricing serves to attract drivers when demand is high; I can't verify the company's intent.&quot; &quot;Fares rose 20% last year&quot;: needs a source (a company report or a regulator or press with a named source) to be Verified; without one, drop it or say &quot;I've heard.&quot; &quot;The map is slow on my phone&quot;: an observation, valid as such, with the device named. &quot;It's the best app in the market&quot;: an opinion; rewrite with a criterion: &quot;It's the one I prefer on speed of pickup.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Find a real source for the fare claim and mark it Verified with the date.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Labels slow you slightly and make the answer defensible.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c28-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C28-03 · Habit metrics from reported figures</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A learning app reports 30 million daily active users, 90 million monthly active users, 6.3 million paid subscribers and that 70% of daily users have a streak of seven days or more. Compute the daily-to-monthly ratio, paid share of monthly users and number of daily users with a seven-day streak. What would you ask next?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Reading company metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Daily-to-monthly: 30 ÷ 90 = 33%. Paid share: 6.3 ÷ 90 = 7%. Seven-day streaks: 0.70 × 30 = 21 million. Next: what share of <em>new</em> users ever reach a seven-day streak (survivorship: the 70% is among those who stayed); whether streak holders learn more (outcome measure); churn by streak length; paid conversion by streak length. The daily-to-monthly ratio suggests a habit product; it does not show learning.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute the implied monthly-only users: 90 − 30 = 60 million at most, an upper bound on those who do not visit daily.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Ratios are quick and need cohort data to mean much.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c28-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C28-04 · Thresholds and base rates</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A wearable alert has two settings per million wearers: Setting 1 notifies 0.8% with a positive predictive value of 0.75; Setting 2 notifies 1.5% with 0.60. Compute true and false alerts for each, and the extra false alerts per extra true alert when moving from Setting 1 to Setting 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Threshold choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Setting 1: 8,000 alerts; 6,000 true; 2,000 false. Setting 2: 15,000 alerts; 9,000 true; 6,000 false. Extra true alerts: 3,000; extra false: 4,000; ratio 1.33 extra false alerts per extra true alert. Whether to move depends on the cost of a false alert against the benefit of an earlier true detection; and on who is alerted (lower prevalence in a younger group makes precision lower there). A defensible default is the conservative setting with an opt-in sensitive mode.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a third setting at 1.1% and 0.68 and plot the tradeoff.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> More sensitivity catches more and costs more false alarms; the weight depends on clinical value.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c28-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C28-05 · Extract a pattern</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> From these two summaries, name a pattern, say where it fails and write a question that applies it to a new product: (a) a messaging app turned encryption on by default; (b) a streaming service set its plan ladder and moved some customers toward the ad plan by raising the price of the ad-free Basic plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Pattern extraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C28-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Pattern:</em> defaults and defaults-by-price steer many users; they act through what a person has to do (nothing) or pay. <em>Evidence:</em> (a) defaults have a large effect on what people end up with (<code>MM-defaults</code>, C07), so a default decides the outcome for many users; (b) a price gap nudges choice. <em>Where it fails:</em> a default wrong for a segment or hard to change; a price nudge that feels like a penalty. <em>Question for a new product:</em> &quot;What happens for the user who never changes anything, and is that what we want for them?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Name the pattern &quot;the cost of choice&quot;: removing a choice improves outcomes for most and removes agency for some.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A pattern speeds you up and can mislead if you stop checking the evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c28-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C28-06 · Teardown of your own product, with the source ladder</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Using FW-C28-01 and FW-C28-02, build a teardown card for a product you use. Find three Verified facts from the top two rungs of the source ladder, and write five claims you must label Reconstructed.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Practice, then a timed 20-minute version (FW-C28-04).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Public sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ninety minutes for the card; twenty minutes for the spoken version.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C28-01, FW-C28-02, FW-C28-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> A good card has: a goal-first statement stated as an assumption; three facts from a filing, a shareholder or investor letter, the company's newsroom or documentation, each with source type and date; strengths with reasons; ranked issues with labels; one change with metric, guardrail and cost; and a pattern with its failure condition. In the spoken version, say &quot;what I can verify&quot; and &quot;what I'm inferring&quot; at least twice, and end with what you could not verify. Check by asking a partner to interrupt with &quot;how do you know that?&quot; at random.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Do the teardown of a competitor and compare the two cards for a shared pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Sourcing takes time and turns a recital into evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c28-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C28-07 · Spot the unsupported claims</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Find and rewrite the unsupported claims: &quot;The company obviously designed the free tier to make people pay later. Half its users upgrade within a week. The onboarding is confusing. Competitors are far behind.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Label discipline.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C11-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;Obviously designed... to make people pay later&quot;: a claim about intent: &quot;I infer the free tier is meant to build a base that some convert; I can't verify the intent.&quot; &quot;Half its users upgrade within a week&quot;: a number with no source: drop it, or say &quot;I'd want the conversion rate; I haven't seen a source,&quot; and note that half is implausibly high for most freemium products without evidence. &quot;The onboarding is confusing&quot;: an observation if you tried it (say what confused you) and an opinion otherwise. &quot;Competitors are far behind&quot;: an opinion with no criterion: rewrite with a criterion and a comparison you can name.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Replace the number with a real, sourced figure if you can find one.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Rewriting unsupported claims shortens an answer and makes it trustworthy.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c28-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C28-08 · Attrition behind a headline number</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> In the published heart-rhythm study, 2,161 participants were notified; 945 attended the first telehealth visit, 658 had a monitoring patch shipped, and 450 returned an analyzable patch (Verified figures from the teardown). Compute each step as a share of the notified, and say what it means for how much weight to put on the 34% found to have the condition on the patch.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Reading a study's funnel.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> TD-C28-07 and C12.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> First visit: 945 ÷ 2,161 = 43.7%. Patch shipped: 658 ÷ 2,161 = 30.4%. Analyzable patch: 450 ÷ 2,161 = 20.8%. The 34% (and the 0.84 positive predictive value, which was measured in those with simultaneous recordings) describes about a fifth of the notified people, so the rest are unobserved. The direction of any bias is not known: people who felt unwell might be more likely to follow up (raising the observed share), or people with transient episodes might show nothing on a later patch (lowering it). Say what you can and cannot conclude: the figures are informative and measured in a self-selected, partly followed-up group; ask for the follow-up reasons and, if possible, a comparison of those who did and did not return a patch.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute the range of the 34% under extreme assumptions about the unobserved 80%: from about 7% of the notified (0.34 × 0.208, if none of the unobserved had the condition) to about 86% (7% plus the unobserved 79%, if all of them did).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Following up everyone is costly and gives a cleaner estimate; a smaller, fully followed-up sample can be more informative than a large, partly followed-up one.</p>\n<span id=\"c28-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: method and labels.</em> Use IQ-C28-01, IQ-C28-02, EX-C28-01, EX-C28-02 and EX-C28-07. Teardown three products you use with the card, labeling every claim.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: depth and patterns.</em> Use IQ-C28-03 to IQ-C28-09, EX-C28-03 to EX-C28-06. Choose two teardowns from C28.2, answer the matching question aloud in 20 minutes with labels, then extract a pattern that also fits a product of your own.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to interrupt with &quot;How do you know that?&quot; and &quot;Is that a fact or your opinion?&quot; at random and to ask what you could not verify.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I state the purpose as an assumption and the criterion I judged by?</li>\n<li>Did I label company facts Verified (with a source type and date) or Reconstructed?</li>\n<li>Did I separate observation, inference and opinion?</li>\n<li>Did I explain why strengths work?</li>\n<li>Did I rank issues with evidence?</li>\n<li>Did I propose one change with a metric, guardrail and cost?</li>\n<li>Did I name the pattern and where it could fail?</li>\n<li>Did I say what I could not verify?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> Record the date, the product, the claims I had to retract or re-label, the metric and cost I gave, my score and the one fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c28-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter to products you use and to your own work.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Pick three products from different domains. For each, write a goal-first statement and find three facts from the top two rungs of the source ladder, with dates.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Build a full teardown card (FW-C28-01) for one product, then give it aloud in twenty minutes (FW-C28-04) to a partner who interrupts with &quot;how do you know that?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Extract one pattern (FW-C28-03) from two of your teardowns and test it on a third product. Teardown a feature of your own product the same way, with your own data labeled as such.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> three labeled teardown cards (evidence of structured product thinking), one pattern with its failure condition (evidence of transferable judgment), and a teardown of your own product's feature with a metric and a cost (a time you critiqued with evidence). Keep every company claim sourced and dated.</p>\n<span id=\"c28-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c28-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A teardown is judged by how you separate what you know from what you think: label company facts Verified (with a source type and date) or Reconstructed, and your critique as observation, inference or opinion.</li>\n<li>State the product's purpose as an assumption first, explain why each strength works, rank issues by impact and fixability, and propose one change with a metric, a guardrail and a cost.</li>\n<li>Prefer primary sources (filings, shareholder letters, newsrooms, peer-reviewed papers) over commentary, and distinguish relative from absolute figures; &quot;+70%&quot; says nothing without a base.</li>\n<li>Patterns recur across products (an engineered first moment, defaults that carry the product, a rhythm that builds a habit, local context that needs a different model, error costs that set thresholds, tiers as design, bold changes needing reversibility); use them as questions to ask a new product, with their failure conditions.</li>\n<li>Use the library to practice, not to recite: form your own view from your own use, and say plainly what you could not verify.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c28-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Peer-reviewed paper: Marco Perez and colleagues, &quot;Large-Scale Assessment of a Smartwatch to Identify Atrial Fibrillation,&quot; <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em> (2019). The source for TD-C28-07 <code>[V: Perez et al.]</code>.</li>\n<li>Netflix quarterly shareholder letters for 2022 and 2023, filed with the US Securities and Exchange Commission. Sources for TD-C28-03 <code>[V: Netflix shareholder letters]</code>.</li>\n<li>Duolingo's registration statement (2021) and annual report for fiscal 2024 filed with the US Securities and Exchange Commission, and its investor site. Sources for TD-C28-01 <code>[V: Duolingo S-1 and annual report]</code>.</li>\n<li>Apple's newsroom announcements of September and December 2016 on AirPods. Sources for TD-C28-06 <code>[V: Apple newsroom, AirPods]</code>.</li>\n<li>The Signal Foundation's April 2016 post on WhatsApp's encryption rollout, and press reports on its spam results. Sources for TD-C28-05 <code>[V: Signal Foundation, April 2016]</code>.</li>\n<li>Airbnb's 2022 and 2023 Summer Release posts and media guide, Skift's September 2025 article on the end of category search, an Airbnb Community reply, and Enterpret's 2023 blog post. Sources for CS-C28-02 (confirmed in October 2026).</li>\n<li>Chapter C11 of this book on the critique and teardown method, and the product teardowns you write yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c29\"></span>\n<h2>C29 — Anti-Patterns</h2>\n<span id=\"c29-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Tell me about a product decision that didn't work.&quot; &quot;A product has 40 million registered users and very short sessions; is it healthy?&quot; &quot;Your team shipped on time and the metric didn't move; what do you do?&quot; &quot;What are your common mistakes, and how do you catch them?&quot; Interviewers ask about failure to learn how you reason about it, and because early-career PMs repeat a small set of mistakes. This chapter is not a second list of mistakes; every chapter already has one. It organizes the recurring ones by root cause, gives a <em>detection signal</em> for each that you can measure in your own work and mock interviews, and gives a fix with a pointer to where the method is taught. Its use is diagnosis: finding which of these you are most likely to make and catching it early.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code>: behavioral questions about failure and learning, execution questions about missed outcomes, and critique questions about why a product failed, at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is the work of retrospectives and reviews.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether you can name a failure pattern, say how you would have detected it earlier, and describe a changed practice, without defensiveness <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for specific signals, causes beyond bad luck, a fix that is a habit and not a resolution, and evidence that you apply it.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You name the pattern, not only the event.</li>\n<li>You identify a root cause that is within your control.</li>\n<li>You state the signal that would have shown it earlier.</li>\n<li>You describe a fix as a changed practice with a way to check it.</li>\n<li>You scan your own work and mocks for your recurring patterns.</li>\n<li>You do not apply a pattern label to someone else's decision without evidence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end failure answers: &quot;it was a timing problem&quot; with no cause, a blamed partner, a lesson that is a platitude, a fix with no check, a list of virtues to avoid vices, and a catalog recited as if it were self-knowledge.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<p>An <strong>anti-pattern</strong> is a common response to a recurring problem that looks sensible, is easy to adopt and reliably produces a worse result than the alternatives. The word names the response, not the person: good PMs fall into anti-patterns because each one solves a short-term pressure.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-c29-1-five-root-cause-clusters\"></span>\n<h4>C29.1 Five root-cause clusters</h4>\n<p>Most of the anti-patterns below grow from five roots (FW-C29-02). Knowing the root predicts where else you are likely to slip.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cluster</th>\n<th>Root</th>\n<th>What it feels like from inside</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>A. Activity mistaken for progress</strong></td>\n<td>Output is visible and outcomes are slow</td>\n<td>&quot;We shipped a lot this quarter&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>B. Optimizing the measure</strong></td>\n<td>What is measured becomes the goal (Goodhart, C05)</td>\n<td>&quot;The number went up&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>C. Avoiding discomfort</strong></td>\n<td>Disagreement, uncertainty, being wrong and saying no feel costly</td>\n<td>&quot;Let's not rock the boat&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>D. Pattern-matching instead of reasoning</strong></td>\n<td>Borrowing a framework, a competitor or a precedent in place of the situation</td>\n<td>&quot;That's what they did&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>E. Not closing the loop</strong></td>\n<td>No check of whether the decision worked</td>\n<td>&quot;We moved on&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Reading the clusters.</strong> If your anti-patterns cluster in A, your fix is outcome measures set before work starts; in B, a guardrail and a second metric; in C, scripts and pre-wiring (C32); in D, first-principles questions about the specific user; in E, a review date and a retest (C34.5).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked about a failure, a candidate says: &quot;We missed the date because engineering underestimated the work.&quot; The interviewer asks what the candidate could have done differently. The candidate has named an event and a cause outside their control, and has no pattern, no signal and no fix. A better answer: &quot;We committed dates for 14 items against about 48 effective engineer-weeks and had 95 weeks of estimates, so we were committed at about twice capacity; the signal I missed was that the plan had no buffer; I now commit to outcomes and ranges and keep 30% unallocated.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>When not to use the catalog.</strong> Do not use it to label other people's decisions in a meeting (&quot;that's a feature factory&quot;); labels end conversations. Some anti-patterns are right in context: a big-bang launch can be correct for a fixed legal date, and building before testing can be correct when a test costs more than the build. Use the catalog on yourself, and ask whether the context makes the pattern a deliberate choice.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-c29-2-strategy-anti-patterns\"></span>\n<h4>C29.2 Strategy anti-patterns</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Anti-pattern</th>\n<th>Looks like</th>\n<th>Why it happens</th>\n<th>Detection signal</th>\n<th>Fix (taught in)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Feature factory</strong></td>\n<td>The team is judged by features shipped, not outcomes changed</td>\n<td>Output is easy to count and to show (A)</td>\n<td>Fewer than half of roadmap items have an outcome metric; few shipped items are measured afterward</td>\n<td>Attach a metric and a review date to every item; report outcomes (C05, C15)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vanity metric as goal</strong></td>\n<td>Registered users, downloads or page views are the headline</td>\n<td>They rise reliably and flatter (B)</td>\n<td>The metric rises while retention or revenue is flat; no decision would change if it fell</td>\n<td>A metric tied to value, with a guardrail (C05)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Solving a symptom</strong></td>\n<td>A fix for a visible problem with no stated cause</td>\n<td>Pressure to act; no problem statement (A, D)</td>\n<td>No written problem statement; &quot;we need X&quot; with no evidence</td>\n<td>The problem-definition template (C02.7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Competing on a rival's terms</strong></td>\n<td>Copying a leader's feature set or strategy</td>\n<td>It is safe and easy to justify (D)</td>\n<td>The only argument is &quot;they have it&quot;; no segment where you would win</td>\n<td>Positioning against a real alternative and a right to win (C18)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strategy by analogy</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;X did it and it worked&quot;</td>\n<td>Pattern-matching on survivors (D)</td>\n<td>No explanation of why the conditions match; only successes cited</td>\n<td>Ask how X differs from us and who failed doing the same (C12 survivorship)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The roadmap as a promise</strong></td>\n<td>Dated feature lists shared as commitments</td>\n<td>Stakeholders want certainty (C)</td>\n<td>Committed work exceeds effective capacity; dates slip and no one is surprised</td>\n<td>Commit to outcomes and ranges, with a buffer (C15, C16)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chasing the loudest customer</strong></td>\n<td>The biggest deal or the loudest voice sets the roadmap</td>\n<td>Revenue pressure; avoiding no (C)</td>\n<td>One account's request outranks data from many; custom work accumulates</td>\n<td>The no script with a path to yes (C32.7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HiPPO decisions</strong></td>\n<td>The <strong>HiPPO</strong> (the highest-paid person's opinion) decides</td>\n<td>Deference; no agreed evidence rule (C)</td>\n<td>Decisions reverse when a senior person changes their mind; tests are not run on senior ideas</td>\n<td>A decision rule agreed before the test; disagree and commit (C14, C32.6)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-c29-3-discovery-anti-patterns\"></span>\n<h4>C29.3 Discovery anti-patterns</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Anti-pattern</th>\n<th>Looks like</th>\n<th>Why it happens</th>\n<th>Detection signal</th>\n<th>Fix (taught in)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Asking users for solutions</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;What features would you like?&quot;</td>\n<td>It is easy and feels like listening (A)</td>\n<td>Interview notes list requests, not situations or behavior</td>\n<td>Ask about the last time they did the job; observe (C06)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Validating with friends or the converted</strong></td>\n<td>Feedback from people who like you or already use you</td>\n<td>Convenience; survivorship (D)</td>\n<td>Sample is mostly known contacts or active users; no one who left or never joined</td>\n<td>Recruit from the target segment, including lapsed users (C06, C26)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Confirmation-seeking research</strong></td>\n<td>Questions framed to get yes</td>\n<td>Attachment to the idea (B)</td>\n<td>Most questions lead; no disconfirming evidence recorded</td>\n<td>State what would change your mind first; log disconfirming evidence (C26)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Skipping the riskiest assumption</strong></td>\n<td>Testing what is safe to test</td>\n<td>The risky test is uncomfortable (C)</td>\n<td>The assumption map has no ranked entry that was tested</td>\n<td>Rank by impact and uncertainty; test the top first (C26.4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interest read as demand</strong></td>\n<td>A waiting list or &quot;I'd buy that&quot;</td>\n<td>Cheap signals are tempting (B, D)</td>\n<td>Sign-ups without price, deposit or repeat use</td>\n<td>Move up the commitment ladder (C26.5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deciding on tiny samples</strong></td>\n<td>A reading from 20 users or one week</td>\n<td>Pressure for speed (A)</td>\n<td>No interval; effect reversed when the sample grew</td>\n<td>Sample size and intervals before the test (C12, C14)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Discovery done once</strong></td>\n<td>A study at kickoff, never repeated</td>\n<td>Process treated as a phase (E)</td>\n<td>The latest user conversation is older than a quarter</td>\n<td>A standing weekly user contact (C06)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-c29-4-execution-anti-patterns\"></span>\n<h4>C29.4 Execution anti-patterns</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Anti-pattern</th>\n<th>Looks like</th>\n<th>Why it happens</th>\n<th>Detection signal</th>\n<th>Fix (taught in)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scope creep</strong></td>\n<td>Requests added mid-flight with no tradeoff</td>\n<td>Saying no is uncomfortable (C)</td>\n<td>Scope changes without a corresponding date or cut</td>\n<td>Written change requests with the tradeoff (C15.7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>No definition of done or non-goals</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;Build it and we'll see&quot;</td>\n<td>Ambiguity feels flexible (C)</td>\n<td>Disputes at review about whether it is finished</td>\n<td>PRD with non-goals and acceptance criteria (C16)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Big-bang launch</strong></td>\n<td>Everything to everyone at once</td>\n<td>Excitement; pressure of a date (A)</td>\n<td>No flag, no staged plan, no rollback trigger</td>\n<td>Staged rollout and rollback triggers (C17)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shipped counted as success</strong></td>\n<td>The launch is the milestone</td>\n<td>Output is visible (A)</td>\n<td>No post-launch review; metrics checked once or never</td>\n<td>Success criteria and a review date set before launch (C17, C20.8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Technical debt ignored until it blocks</strong></td>\n<td>Quality work is always next quarter</td>\n<td>Features are rewarded more visibly (A, B)</td>\n<td>Rising incident rate; estimates growing for similar work</td>\n<td>A standing share of capacity and a list of known debt with its cost per sprint (C16.4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The spec as a handoff</strong></td>\n<td>A document thrown over the wall</td>\n<td>Writing replaces conversation (A)</td>\n<td>Engineering questions arrive after the build starts</td>\n<td>Working sessions with engineering and design before and during (C16)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Consensus as the goal</strong></td>\n<td>Everyone must agree</td>\n<td>Conflict avoidance (C)</td>\n<td>Decisions slow; no one owns the call</td>\n<td>Decision rights; one decider (C01.1, C32.6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Over-polishing before learning</strong></td>\n<td>Weeks refining a version no one has tried</td>\n<td>Perfectionism; fear (C)</td>\n<td>No users have seen it after weeks</td>\n<td>Smallest testable version; a time box (C15, C26)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-c29-5-communication-anti-patterns\"></span>\n<h4>C29.5 Communication anti-patterns</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Anti-pattern</th>\n<th>Looks like</th>\n<th>Why it happens</th>\n<th>Detection signal</th>\n<th>Fix (taught in)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Burying the lede</strong></td>\n<td>Background first, point last</td>\n<td>Chronology is easy (D)</td>\n<td>Readers ask &quot;what do you need from me?&quot;</td>\n<td>Answer first (C32.1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Status as activity</strong></td>\n<td>A list of meetings and tasks</td>\n<td>It shows effort (A)</td>\n<td>No reader can tell the status or the ask</td>\n<td>Headline, change, next, need (C32.2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The surprise in the meeting</strong></td>\n<td>A first objection in the room</td>\n<td>No pre-wiring (C)</td>\n<td>Decisions postponed after the meeting</td>\n<td>Pre-wire the objectors one-to-one (C32.3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>All &quot;we&quot;</strong></td>\n<td>No personal decision or action visible</td>\n<td>Modesty, or hiding (C)</td>\n<td>A story with no &quot;I&quot; and no decision</td>\n<td>Decision and action in the first person with credit (C31)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hedging without numbers</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;Probably maybe&quot;</td>\n<td>Fear of being wrong (C)</td>\n<td>No probability, range or check date</td>\n<td>The uncertainty statement (C32.8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Winning the argument, losing the partner</strong></td>\n<td>Correct and corrosive</td>\n<td>Ego; short-term focus (B)</td>\n<td>Partners stop sharing information early</td>\n<td>Steelman, agree criteria, commit (C32.6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bad news held back</strong></td>\n<td>Waiting for a fix before telling</td>\n<td>Fear of reaction (C)</td>\n<td>Leaders learn from someone else</td>\n<td>Tell early with impact and plan (C32.5)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-c29-6-interview-specific-anti-patterns\"></span>\n<h4>C29.6 Interview-specific anti-patterns</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Anti-pattern</th>\n<th>Looks like</th>\n<th>Why it happens</th>\n<th>Detection signal</th>\n<th>Fix (taught in)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Framework recitation</strong></td>\n<td>A named framework with no user and no decision</td>\n<td>Anxiety; memorization (D)</td>\n<td>The answer could fit any prompt</td>\n<td>Reason from the user and the decision (C03, C07)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Jumping to solutions</strong></td>\n<td>Features in the first minute</td>\n<td>Eagerness (A)</td>\n<td>No user or problem stated before ideas</td>\n<td>Clarify, define the user and the problem (C07)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metrics without guardrails</strong></td>\n<td>One success metric</td>\n<td>Habit (B)</td>\n<td>No counter-metric named</td>\n<td>Primary, guardrail and decision rule (C05)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Answering a different question</strong></td>\n<td>A prepared story that does not fit</td>\n<td>Over-rehearsal (D)</td>\n<td>The interviewer re-asks the question</td>\n<td>Answer what was asked; say if you pivot (C31)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inventing under probing</strong></td>\n<td>A number that changes</td>\n<td>Pressure to look knowledgeable (C)</td>\n<td>A figure differs between answers</td>\n<td>Say &quot;I don't recall&quot;; give a range (C31.7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Overclaiming company knowledge</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;They obviously did it because...&quot;</td>\n<td>Wanting to sound informed (D)</td>\n<td>No source for a company claim</td>\n<td>Verified, Reconstructed, Opinion labels (C28)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Practicing the favorite round</strong></td>\n<td>Hours on what you already do well</td>\n<td>It feels good (A)</td>\n<td>Mock scores flat in the weakest dimension</td>\n<td>Importance times gap; re-score (C33.14, C34.7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reading a bad round as a verdict</strong></td>\n<td>Spiraling into the next round</td>\n<td>Fear (C)</td>\n<td>Scores drop after one weak round</td>\n<td>Reset routine; log and fix (C34.8)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-c29-7-detection-signals-and-fixes-the-anti-pattern-scan\"></span>\n<h4>C29.7 Detection signals and fixes: the anti-pattern scan</h4>\n<p>A catalog helps only if you check yourself against it (FW-C29-01). The scan has four steps.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Collect evidence.</strong> Use your artifacts: the last three documents you wrote (a spec, an update, a decision document); your roadmap and its outcome measures; your mock scorecards and error log (C34); and your stories. Evidence beats memory.</li>\n<li><strong>Match signals.</strong> For each artifact, run down the detection signals in the tables and mark the ones present. Count how often each appears across artifacts.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the cluster.</strong> Group the marked patterns by root cause (A to E). One cluster that dominates is your leverage point.</li>\n<li><strong>Fix with a practice and a retest.</strong> Choose one practice (for example, &quot;every roadmap item gets an outcome metric and a review date&quot;), apply it to the next artifact, and check the signal again in three weeks.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fixes are habits, not intentions.</strong> A fix is credible when it names the practice, the artifact where it will appear and the check. &quot;I'll communicate better&quot; fails; &quot;I now send a four-line update with an explicit ask every Friday, and replies rose from 12% to 60%&quot; works (CS-C32-01).</p>\n<p><strong>The interview use.</strong> When asked about a failure, name the pattern, give its detection signal, describe the practice that replaced it and cite the check. The structure is: <em>what happened, the pattern, the root cause in my control, the signal I missed, the practice I changed, and the evidence it works.</em></p>\n<span id=\"c29-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Three frameworks, set out below. The metric card (FW-C05-01), the experiment decision rules (FW-C14-05), the scope-negotiation script (FW-C15-07), the launch-or-hold table (FW-C17-04), the uncertainty statement (FW-C32-08), the disagreement protocol (FW-C32-07), the story anatomy (FW-C31-03), the answer-journal loop (FW-C34-04) and the weakest-competency diagnosis (FW-C34-06) are the fixes the tables point to and are not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c29-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C29-01 · The anti-pattern scan · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> three or more of your own artifacts (a spec, an update, a roadmap with its outcome measures, mock scorecards and the error log, your stories).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the detection signals matched in each artifact, a count by anti-pattern, the dominant root-cause cluster, one practice chosen as the fix with the artifact where it will appear, and a retest date with the signal to recheck.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you scan evidence, not memory.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a scan takes an hour and replaces a vague sense of improving with a measured change.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> scanning only one artifact; choosing several fixes; no retest.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked examples 1 and 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Artifact</th>\n<th>Signals matched</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Cluster</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c29-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C29-02 · The root-cause clusters · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a set of marked anti-patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> each pattern assigned to a cluster (A activity mistaken for progress; B optimizing the measure; C avoiding discomfort; D pattern-matching instead of reasoning; E not closing the loop), the dominant cluster, and the fix that fits it (A: outcome measures first; B: a guardrail and a second metric; C: scripts and pre-wiring; D: first-principles questions about the specific user; E: a review date and a retest).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> patterns have a common cause you can address once.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> clustering simplifies and may misassign a pattern with several causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating each pattern separately; a fix aimed at the wrong cluster.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c29-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C29-03 · The failure-story structure · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a decision that did not work.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer in six parts: what happened (with the cost); the pattern; the root cause within your control; the signal you missed; the practice you changed; the evidence that it works.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can tell it without blame and with numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a structured failure story is honest and can sound rehearsed if it has no specifics.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> an event with no pattern; a cause outside your control; a fix with no check.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What happened</td>\n<td>The decision and its cost, with a number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The pattern</td>\n<td>The named anti-pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Root cause in my control</td>\n<td>A choice you made, not a circumstance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The signal I missed</td>\n<td>What would have shown it earlier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The practice I changed</td>\n<td>A habit, with the artifact where it appears</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The evidence</td>\n<td>A before-and-after measure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c29-the-tell-me-about-a-decision-that-didn-t-work-answer-for-thr\"></span>\n<h4>The &quot;tell me about a decision that didn't work&quot; answer, for three minutes</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Seconds</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What happened and what it cost</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>The decision, the stakes, one number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The pattern and the root cause</td>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>The anti-pattern named; a cause in your control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The signal you missed</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>A measurable signal that was available</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The practice you changed</td>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>A habit and the artifact where it shows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The evidence</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>A before-and-after measure; one use since</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> Keep it to about three minutes and expect probes: &quot;why didn't you see it?&quot; and &quot;how do you know it works?&quot; Prepare numbers for both.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> &quot;The pattern was a feature factory: we measured shipping, not outcomes. The signal I missed was that only 6 of 20 roadmap items had an outcome metric.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> &quot;Now every item has a metric and a review date, and last quarter 90% of shipped items were reviewed, up from 21%.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c29-type-failure-learning-or-mistakes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: failure, learning or mistakes</h4>\n<p>The failure-story structure with a named pattern and a measured fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-type-why-did-a-product-fail\"></span>\n<h4>Type: why did a product fail</h4>\n<p>Hypotheses by family (strategy, discovery, execution, communication), evidence for each, the most likely root cause and what you would have measured.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-type-a-team-is-stuck-or-a-metric-is-flat\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a team is stuck or a metric is flat</h4>\n<p>Scan for activity-versus-outcome, optimizing the wrong measure, avoided decisions and an unclosed loop.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-type-what-are-your-weaknesses-or-recurring-errors\"></span>\n<h4>Type: what are your weaknesses or recurring errors</h4>\n<p>Your top pattern from a scan, the signal, the practice and its check.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What could you have done differently?&quot;</td>\n<td>Root cause in your control</td>\n<td>Name a choice you made and the alternative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How would you have known earlier?&quot;</td>\n<td>A detection signal</td>\n<td>A measurable signal that existed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How do you know it works now?&quot;</td>\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>A before-and-after number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Isn't that just bad luck?&quot;</td>\n<td>Pattern versus event</td>\n<td>Name the pattern and show it recurs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Is that a fix or a resolution?&quot;</td>\n<td>Habit versus intention</td>\n<td>The practice, the artifact and the check</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the teams and figures are invented (invented), and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-worked-example-1-scanning-a-roadmap-for-a-feature-factory-il\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Scanning a roadmap for a feature factory (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The artifact (FW-C29-01).</strong> A team's half-year roadmap has 20 items (invented). Six items carry an outcome metric with a target. Fourteen items have shipped; of those, three have been measured against a target after launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Match the signals.</strong> <em>Feature factory:</em> outcome coverage is 6 of 20 (30%); shipped-and-measured is 3 of 14 (21%). Fewer than half of items have an outcome metric (below the threshold in C29.2), and about four in five shipped items were never evaluated. Of the whole roadmap, 3 of 20 items (15%) have closed the loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Read it.</strong> The team is organized around output. The cluster is A (activity mistaken for progress), with E (not closing the loop) behind it: items are launched and forgotten, so the team cannot know which of its 14 launches mattered.</p>\n<p><strong>The fix (practice and retest).</strong> For every new item, write the outcome metric, its baseline and its target in the item before work starts; set a review date four weeks after launch; keep a one-page outcomes log. For the 11 shipped items with no review, run a quick review this month (a metric check, not a project). Retest in six weeks: target at least 90% outcome coverage on new items and at least 80% of launched items reviewed on time.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Two ratios from a roadmap turned &quot;we feel busy&quot; into a measured pattern, a cluster and a practice with a retest.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-worked-example-2-a-registered-users-number-and-a-session-dis\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — A registered-users number and a session distribution (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A social product reports 40 million registered users. A product manager looks at sessions: of 1,000,000 sessions in a day, 90% last about 3 seconds and 10% last about 300 seconds (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Compute.</strong> The mean session length: (900,000 × 3 + 100,000 × 300) ÷ 1,000,000 = 32.7 seconds. The median is 3 seconds. The 10% of long sessions account for 30,000,000 of 32,700,000 seconds, about 92% of all session time.</p>\n<p><strong>Read it.</strong> The mean, 33 seconds, looks like a modest but real product; the median, 3 seconds, shows that most sessions are accidental or abandoned, and a small minority carries nearly all the use. A registered-user count says nothing about this (a vanity metric, C29.2), and an average hides it (survivorship and distribution, C12).</p>\n<p><strong>What to measure instead.</strong> The share of users with at least three sessions of 30 seconds or more in a week; weekly active users who return in each of four consecutive weeks (a retention curve by cohort, C05); the number of people who create or share something. Percentiles (median, 90th) beside the mean. And the origin of the short sessions: if they come from links or integrations in other products, they are not interest.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> If fewer than, say, 10% of registered users meet the engagement bar, the product's problem is not growth; it is a missing reason to return, and registered-user growth should not be reported as success.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A distribution revealed what a mean and a count concealed, and gave a better metric and a decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-worked-example-3-the-roadmap-as-a-promise-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — The roadmap as a promise (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C29-01).</strong> A team of 5 engineers plans a 12-quarter-week cycle (12 weeks). Capacity is 5 × 12 = 60 engineer-weeks. About 20% goes to unplanned work (incidents, support), so effective capacity is 48 engineer-weeks. The roadmap shared with sales commits dates to items whose estimates total 95 engineer-weeks (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Compute.</strong> Committed work against effective capacity: 95 ÷ 48 = 198%; against gross capacity, 95 ÷ 60 = 158%. The team is committed to about twice what it can do.</p>\n<p><strong>Predict what happens.</strong> Dates slip; the team cuts quality or works overtime; sales loses trust; and each slip is explained as bad luck. The pattern is the roadmap as a promise (C29.2), clusters C (avoiding the uncomfortable no) and A.</p>\n<p><strong>The fix.</strong> Commit to outcomes and ranges, not dated features: share a &quot;now, next, later&quot; view with confidence levels; commit only to about 70% of effective capacity (about 34 engineer-weeks) and hold the rest as buffer; make a published tradeoff when something is added. Where a dated promise is needed (a legal deadline), commit to that item and drop others. Retest: percentage of committed items delivered in the cycle, and the number of date changes communicated late.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A capacity calculation made the over-commitment visible before the first slip, and the fix traded certainty about features for certainty about outcomes.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-worked-example-4-scanning-your-mock-log-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Scanning your mock log (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The artifact.</strong> Eight mock interviews (invented), with errors tagged from the scorecards and the error log (C34.5). Counts of mocks in which each error appeared: no guardrail metric, 6; no number in the result, 5; ran out of time before risks, 4; jumped to a solution, 3; all &quot;we&quot; in a story, 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Compute frequencies.</strong> No guardrail: 6 of 8 (75%); no number: 5 of 8 (63%); ran out of time: 4 of 8 (50%); jumped to a solution: 3 of 8 (38%); all &quot;we&quot;: 2 of 8 (25%).</p>\n<p><strong>Find the cluster.</strong> &quot;No guardrail&quot; and &quot;no number&quot; are both cluster B (optimizing the measure, naming a metric without its counter-metric) and cluster E (not closing the loop with a result); &quot;ran out of time&quot; is cluster A (activity, over-long setup); &quot;jumped to a solution&quot; is cluster D. Clusters B and E account for 11 of 20 error instances.</p>\n<p><strong>The fix.</strong> One practice for the dominant pattern: after naming any primary metric, say its guardrail in the same sentence; and one drill: the metric-definition drill (C34.6), ten reps. Retest in four mocks: target no-guardrail errors in at most 1 of 4 (25%).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Counting errors across mocks turned &quot;I'm weak on metrics&quot; into a specific, measured pattern, a cluster and a retestable practice.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-worked-example-5-a-failure-story-structured-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — A failure story, structured (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Tell me about a decision that didn't work.&quot; The story and its figures are invented (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>The answer (FW-C29-03), as spoken.</strong> &quot;Last year I led a quarter for our onboarding team where we shipped fourteen changes and only one moved completion, by about 1.5%. The pattern was a feature factory: we measured shipping, not outcomes. The root cause was in my control: I accepted the roadmap as a list of features and didn't ask for a metric on each. The signal I missed was available: when I counted afterwards, only six of twenty roadmap items had an outcome metric, and only three of fourteen shipped items had been reviewed. What I changed: every item now carries a metric, a baseline, a target and a review date before work starts, and I keep an outcomes log. The evidence: in the next quarter, outcome coverage on new items was 100% and 85% of launched items were reviewed on time; we also stopped two items after the first review because the metric was not moving, which saved about three engineer-weeks. The cost of the original quarter was roughly a quarter of the team's output that did not change a metric.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Probes to prepare.</strong> &quot;How did you pick the one that moved?&quot; (it had a metric; I attribute it with a holdout). &quot;Why didn't you see it earlier?&quot; (I looked at velocity, which looked fine). &quot;What would you do if a stakeholder insisted on an item with no metric?&quot; (ask what outcome it serves; propose a lightweight metric and a review date).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The six-part structure gave a failure story with a pattern, a signal, a habit and evidence, which survives probing.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Tell me about a time a product decision didn't work.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c29-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;We launched a feature that users didn't adopt. It was a tough market and the timing wasn't right. I learned that I need to listen to users more.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no pattern, a cause outside the candidate's control, no signal, no practice, no evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;We shipped a sharing feature that only 2% of users tried. I had assumed demand from a few customer requests and hadn't tested it. The mistake was building from loud requests without evidence of broader need. What I do now is test the riskiest assumption first with a small prototype before committing engineering time, and I check requests against usage data.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c29-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;The pattern was asking users for solutions and treating a handful of requests as demand. The signal I missed was that all six requests came from large accounts, which were 4% of users; I hadn't checked who was asking. My practice changed in three ways: I record, with each request, how many users it represents; I run a smoke test or a manual version before building (the next feature, we tested a fake door for two weeks, and a 0.8% click rate against a 4% threshold stopped us, saving about eight engineer-weeks); and I set a kill criterion before starting. The evidence is that two of my last three proposals were stopped or reshaped at the test stage, and the one we built reached 11% adoption in its first month. The cost of the original feature was about six engineer-weeks and a quarter's goodwill with the team.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c29-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Owns a cause in their control</td>\n<td>Names the pattern and the missed signal with numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Describes a changed practice</td>\n<td>Shows the practice used since, with evidence and the avoided cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keeps it specific</td>\n<td>Sets kill criteria in advance; separates request from demand</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google has publicly shut down a number of products, and its 2018 statement on the consumer version of Google+ (CS-C29-02) is an example of reading engagement honestly <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google's Project Strobe post of 8 October 2018; closures in CS-C15-02]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions about failures, ambiguity and product decisions with data <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: tell failure stories with a named pattern, a number and a measurable signal, and be ready to discuss engagement distributions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta's published values emphasize moving fast and focusing on long-term impact <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta's careers culture page, six core values]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on speed-versus-quality failures and results with numbers <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: pair &quot;moved fast&quot; stories with the guardrail or staged rollout you added afterward.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft's culture materials emphasize a growth mindset <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft careers culture page]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on learning from mistakes and changing your mind <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: show the practice you changed and how you taught it to others.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon's published principles include ownership and diving deep into data <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles, as cited by several summaries]</code>. Candidates commonly report repeated probing of the detail behind a failure <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: have the numbers for the cost, the missed signal and the result, and own the cause.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple does not publish a hiring rubric; candidates commonly report an emphasis on craft and judgment about what to leave out <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: include a failure of restraint or over-polishing (C29.4) and the discipline you now apply, without disclosing confidential details of past work.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix's published culture document emphasizes candor and context <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on candid feedback and independent judgment <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: tell a failure story that includes the feedback you received and how you acted on it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe is widely described as valuing clear thinking and writing <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: The Pragmatic Engineer, Inside Stripe's Engineering Culture Part 2 (strong culture of writing); Slab interview with Stripe's former Documentation Manager]</code>. Candidates report questions on rigor <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: write the failure story as a short post-mortem with the six parts of FW-C29-03.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c29-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Mistakes in <em>using</em> the catalog (the anti-patterns themselves are the subject of section 2).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Labeling other people's decisions</td>\n<td>&quot;That's a feature factory&quot; in a meeting</td>\n<td>Use the catalog on yourself; ask questions about their evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Reciting the catalog as self-knowledge</td>\n<td>A list of patterns with no personal example</td>\n<td>Scan your artifacts; give one numbered example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Naming an event, not a pattern</td>\n<td>&quot;We missed the date&quot;</td>\n<td>Name the pattern and show it recurs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A cause outside your control</td>\n<td>&quot;Engineering underestimated&quot;</td>\n<td>Find the choice you made</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>A fix that is an intention</td>\n<td>&quot;I'll communicate more&quot;</td>\n<td>A practice, an artifact and a check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Several fixes at once</td>\n<td>A list of resolutions</td>\n<td>One practice for the dominant cluster</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>No retest</td>\n<td>The fix is never checked</td>\n<td>A retest date and the signal to recheck</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Treating a deliberate choice as a pattern</td>\n<td>Criticizing a big-bang launch before a legal deadline</td>\n<td>Ask whether the context made it right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Over-fitting the label</td>\n<td>Seeing a pattern in one data point</td>\n<td>Count across artifacts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Stopping at diagnosis</td>\n<td>A scan with no change</td>\n<td>Apply the fix to the next artifact</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c29-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> is from C05, <a href=\"#gl-survivorship-bias\">survivorship bias</a> from C12 and <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Five whys</strong> (<code>MM-five-whys</code>). Ask why repeatedly until you reach a cause you can change <code>[V: Ohno, Toyota Production System, 1988]</code>. &quot;The date slipped&quot; (why?) &quot;we over-committed&quot; (why?) &quot;we had no capacity view&quot; (why?) &quot;I did not ask for one.&quot; The last answer is the one a fix can use.</p>\n<p><strong>Goodhart's law</strong> (<code>MM-goodhart</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C05). When a measure becomes a target it stops measuring what matters; this is cluster B and the root of vanity metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>Survivorship bias</strong> (<code>MM-survivorship-bias</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-survivorship-bias\">survivorship bias</a>, C12). Reading only the successes or the active users hides the failures and the leavers; it is behind analogy-based strategy and engagement averages.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). Ask how you would guarantee a bad outcome and check whether you are doing it: shipping without metrics, validating only with fans, skipping the riskiest test.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Engineers see anti-patterns early: shifting scope, specs thrown over the wall, debt deferred. Ask them which of these they see most, and treat their answer as data for your scan.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Designers and researchers see discovery anti-patterns: leading questions, friends as participants, interest read as demand. Invite them to review your research plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Data scientists see metric anti-patterns: vanity metrics, averages that hide distributions, tiny samples. Ask them to review your metrics and your reading of results.</li>\n<li><strong>Your manager and peers.</strong> They see communication and decision patterns that you cannot. Ask for one example of each pattern in your work, and use it in your scan.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c29-checklist-before-you-retell-a-failure-or-run-a-retrospective\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you retell a failure or run a retrospective</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Have I named the pattern, not just the event?</li>\n<li>Is the root cause a choice within my control?</li>\n<li>Do I know the signal that would have shown it earlier, and was it available?</li>\n<li>Is the fix a practice with an artifact where it appears?</li>\n<li>Is there a retest date and a signal to check?</li>\n<li>Do I have a before-and-after number and one use since?</li>\n<li>Did I scan across artifacts, not one data point?</li>\n<li>Have I asked whether the context made the pattern a deliberate choice?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c29-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c29-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C29-01 · Fourteen features and one metric moved · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> An onboarding team ships fourteen changes in a half-year and celebrates a record output. In the review, the director asks which changes improved completion. The PM cannot say.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A roadmap built from stakeholder requests; velocity as the team's headline measure; no standing review of launched work; a PM new to the team.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Of 20 roadmap items, six had an outcome metric with a target; 14 shipped; three of the shipped items had been measured afterward. Completion rose from 52% to 53.5% over the half-year; a holdout on one change attributes about 1.5 points to a single item. The team estimates it spent about 90% of its engineering time on the other 13 items.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The team measured shipping, not outcomes, so it cannot tell which of fourteen launches mattered, and only one visibly did.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Applying the scan (FW-C29-01): outcome coverage was 30% and shipped-and-measured 21%, the signals of a feature factory (cluster A) with unclosed loops (E). The root cause within the PM's control was accepting the roadmap as a feature list without insisting on metrics. The fix is a practice with an artifact (the roadmap item) and a check (the share of items with metrics and reviews).</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep velocity as the headline and add features</td>\n<td>Visible output</td>\n<td>More unmeasured launches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Require an outcome metric, baseline, target and review date on every new item; review the shipped items; stop items whose metric does not move</td>\n<td>Evidence of impact</td>\n<td>A slower start per item; some items rejected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Stop shipping for a quarter to analyze</td>\n<td>Learning</td>\n<td>Lost momentum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B adds an hour per item and shows where effort goes; A repeats the pattern; C is too blunt.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. Backfill the metrics for the 11 shipped items without one as a quick review, adopt the item template and an outcomes log, and report outcome coverage and the share of items reviewed alongside velocity.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario, in the next quarter outcome coverage on new items was 100% and 85% of launched items were reviewed on time; two items were stopped after a four-week review because their metrics did not move, freeing about three engineer-weeks (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If stakeholders had resisted metrics on some items, the PM would have labeled them as bets with a lighter metric and a short review date. The lesson: measure the outcome of each launch and close the loop.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c29-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C29-02 · Google+, 2018: low engagement, an API bug and the end of the consumer product · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Google's post of 8 October 2018 announcing the first findings of Project Strobe and the shutdown of consumer Google+, and its post of 10 December 2018, &quot;Expediting changes to Google+&quot;, both read on Google's blog (primary); Google's Workspace blog post of 30 January 2019 on the April 2019 shutdown (primary); and a reference summary of the service's history, which quotes Ars Technica and reports a 2011 user count (secondary). Why Google+ was built and promoted, and how its user counts were compiled, are not stated in the Google posts and are not asserted here. The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> At the beginning of 2018 Google started Project Strobe, a review of third-party developer access to Google account and Android device data. On 8 October 2018 it announced the first findings, among them the shutdown of the consumer version of Google+ through a ten-month wind-down to be completed by the end of the following August <code>[V: Google post, 8 October 2018]</code>. The post said the review had found a bug in one of the Google+ People APIs, discovered and patched in March 2018, under which apps could see profile fields that were shared with the user but not marked public; that the profiles of up to 500,000 Google+ accounts were potentially affected (the company kept the relevant log data for only two weeks); that up to 438 applications may have used the API; and that it found no evidence that any developer was aware of the bug or abused the API, or that any profile data was misused <code>[V: Google post, 8 October 2018]</code>. It also said the review &quot;crystallized what we've known for a while&quot;: Google+ had not achieved broad consumer or developer adoption, had seen limited user interaction with apps, and the consumer version had low usage and engagement, with 90 percent of user sessions under five seconds; it added that Google+ was better suited as an enterprise product and that the company would focus on its enterprise efforts <code>[V: Google post, 8 October 2018]</code>. On 10 December 2018 Google reported a second bug, from a software update in November, that affected about 52.5 million users through a Google+ API; it said it would sunset all Google+ APIs within 90 days and brought the end of consumer Google+ forward from August 2019 to April 2019 <code>[V: Google post, 10 December 2018]</code>. A Google Workspace post of 30 January 2019 gives 2 April 2019 as the date from which consumer accounts would be shut down and content deleted <code>[V: Google Workspace blog, January 2019]</code>. A reference summary reports 90 million users at the end of 2011 and quotes Ars Technica that many sign-ups were incidental to signing up for other Google services <code>[V: reference summary; secondary]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A large company with other products a network could be tied to (an inference); larger established social networks as competitors (an inference); a product that had been promoted for years (an inference); a review that found a bug in a Google+ API and, later in 2018, a second bug; and a decision to shut down a product on which, in the post's words, engineering teams had put effort and dedication.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported by Google: the first bug and its scale, the second bug, the engagement statement, and the wind-down and acceleration dates; and, from a secondary source, a 2011 user count and a remark that many sign-ups were incidental.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> A product's reported user numbers and its actual usage diverged, and Google's own review stated the gap publicly in October 2018. As an observable problem: &quot;What does 'users' mean for a product where most sessions end in seconds?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, through this chapter's tools.</em></p>\n<p><em>Vanity metric and distribution (C29.2; Worked example 2).</em> A statement that 90% of sessions lasted under five seconds describes a distribution in which most sessions are almost empty. A count of accounts or registered users cannot show that, and an average session time would be dominated by the small minority of long sessions. The right questions are about the median, the share of users with repeated meaningful sessions and retention by cohort.</p>\n<p><em>Competing on a rival's terms (C29.2).</em> The product competed with larger established social networks (an inference; the Google posts read do not describe the competition); whether the network could ever have reached the level needed for people to find value is a network-effect question (C18, C19).</p>\n<p><em>What kept it going.</em> The Google posts read do not say why the product was kept for years or how its user counts were compiled. A secondary report quotes Ars Technica saying many sign-ups were incidental to signing up for other Google services, which is the question to ask of any such number: does it count intentional use or incidental exposure?</p>\n<p><em>Closing the loop (C29.1, cluster E).</em> The post says the review &quot;crystallized what we've known for a while&quot; and presents the API bug as underlining the difficulty of maintaining the product; the sources read do not say whether the bug changed the timing of the decision, whether a kill criterion existed, or when low engagement was first recognized inside the company. The December post says that a second bug brought the closure forward.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> The company's internal metrics before 2018, its reasons for continuing the product, or the cost of keeping it. The case supports an analysis of how a headline number can hide the real distribution and how decisions to stop get delayed, not a verdict on the people involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could face; not a record of Google's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep investing to grow users</td>\n<td>A chance at network effects; avoids admitting failure</td>\n<td>Continued cost with weak evidence of use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Set a kill criterion early on engagement (for example, share of users with repeated meaningful sessions) and review it on a date</td>\n<td>Early honest learning</td>\n<td>Earlier, more visible failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Narrow to a segment with real use (here, business use, which Google said it would keep) and stop the consumer service</td>\n<td>Preserves what works</td>\n<td>A smaller product</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B and C cost an earlier admission and save years of spend; A preserves hope and, per the evidence, a low-engagement product.</p>\n";
